(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Data Structures & Algorithms"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Data Structures & Algorithms"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Data Structures and Algorithms: Assignments with Worked Solutions\n\nImplement every data structure yourself before you use the library version. Code is C++17 (compile with `g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra`); the traces and counts below were produced by running the algorithms. Marks are given for correctness, stated complexity, handling of edge cases and clear reasoning.\n\n# Assignment 1: Analysis, Lists, Stacks, Queues and Sorting (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Complexity (12 marks)** (a) Give the exact number of executions of the inner statement and the Θ-class, and evaluate for n = 16 [6]:\n (i) `for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) for (j = 1; j <= i; j++) work();`\n (ii) `for (i = 1; i < n; i *= 2) work();`\n (iii) `for (i = 0; i < n; i++) for (j = 1; j < n; j *= 2) work();` (Θ-class only)\n(b) Solve with the master theorem: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n; T(n) = 4T(n/2) + n; T(n) = T(n/2) + 1; T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n^2; T(n) = 3T(n/2) + n [5]. (c) Is 3n^2 + 10n + 7 in O(n^2)? In Ω(n)? In Θ(n^3)? [1]\n\n**Q2. Amortised analysis (8 marks)** (a) A dynamic array starts with capacity 1 and doubles when full. Count the elements copied during 17 pushes and compare with 2n [4]. (b) A binary counter starts at 0 and is incremented 16 times; count the total bit flips and give the amortised cost [4].\n\n**Q3. Linked lists (10 marks)** Write (a) `Node* reverse(Node*)`, (b) `bool hasCycle(Node*)` using two pointers, (c) `Node* mergeSorted(Node*, Node*)`. Give the complexities and the results of reversing 1→2→3→4→5 and merging 1→4→7 with 2→3→9.\n\n**Q4. Stacks and queues (10 marks)** (a) Convert `3 + 4 * 2 / ( 1 - 5 ) ^ 2` to postfix (the operator ^ is right-associative) and evaluate it [5]. (b) A circular queue has capacity 5. Starting empty (head = 0), perform enqueue 1, 2, 3, 4; dequeue, dequeue; enqueue 5, 6, 7, 8. Give the return of each operation and the final contents of the array, head and size [5].\n\n**Q5. Sorting (14 marks)** (a) For [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6] count the comparisons (and shifts or swaps) of insertion sort, selection sort and bubble sort (with early exit) [6]. (b) Show the result of the first Lomuto partition (last element as pivot) of [3, 8, 2, 5, 1, 4, 7, 6] and the number of partition calls to finish the sort [3]. (c) Build a max-heap from [4, 10, 3, 5, 1] [2]. (d) Counting sort on [4, 2, 2, 8, 3, 3, 1]: give the count array and the cumulative counts [3].\n\n**Q6. Searching and bounds (6 marks)** (a) Show how many probes binary search needs in the worst case for n = 1,000,000 [2]. (b) Give the information-theoretic lower bound on comparisons to sort 8 items [2]. (c) Explain why a comparison sort cannot beat Ω(n log n) but counting sort can [2].\n\n**Q7. Merge sort (10 marks)** Write a stable merge sort in C++ that counts comparisons, state the recurrence, and report the comparison counts for [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6] and for the sorted array 1..8.\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a)(i) Step 1: for each i the inner loop runs i times, so the total is 1 + 2 + ... + n.\nStep 2: the sum is **n(n + 1)/2**, which is Θ(n^2).\nStep 3: for n = 16: 16 × 17/2 = **136**.\n(ii) Step 1: i takes the values 1, 2, 4, 8, ... while i < n (it doubles each time).\nStep 2: the number of iterations is ⌈log2 n⌉: **Θ(log n)**.\nStep 3: for n = 16: i = 1, 2, 4, 8 (16 is not < 16), so **4**.\n(iii) Step 1: the inner loop runs ⌈log2 n⌉ times (j doubles).\nStep 2: it is executed for each of the n outer iterations: n ⌈log2 n⌉ times, **Θ(n log n)**.\n(b) Master theorem T(n) = a T(n/b) + Θ(n^d): compare d with log_b a.\n(1) a = 2, b = 2, d = 1: log_2 2 = 1 = d, so **Θ(n log n)**.\n(2) a = 4, b = 2, d = 1: log_2 4 = 2 > 1, so **Θ(n^2)**.\n(3) a = 1, b = 2, d = 0: log_2 1 = 0 = d, so **Θ(log n)**.\n(4) a = 2, b = 2, d = 2: log_2 2 = 1 < 2, so **Θ(n^2)**.\n(5) a = 3, b = 2, d = 1: log_2 3 = 1.585 > 1, so **Θ(n^1.585)**.\n(c) Step 1: 3n^2 + 10n + 7 ≤ 4n^2 when n^2 - 10n - 7 ≥ 0, true for n ≥ 11 (121 - 110 - 7 = 4), so it is in O(n^2) with C = 4.\nStep 2: it is at least n for n ≥ 1, so it is in Ω(n): **yes**.\nStep 3: it is **not** in Θ(n^3): n^3 grows faster, so it is O(n^3) but not Ω(n^3).\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Capacity starts at 1 and doubles when a push finds the array full. A table of the pushes that copy:\nPush 1: size 0 to 1, no copy (capacity 1).\nPush 2: array full (size 1), copy 1 element, capacity 2.\nPush 3: full (size 2), copy 2, capacity 4.\nPush 5: full (size 4), copy 4, capacity 8.\nPush 9: full (size 8), copy 8, capacity 16.\nPush 17: full (size 16), copy 16, capacity 32.\nTotal copies = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = **31** < 2n = 34. In general the copies form a geometric series less than 2n, so the **amortised cost per push is O(1)** (at most about 3 element writes: one for the push and up to two for copying).\n(b) Flips at increment k = (number of trailing 1s of k - 1) + 1 (the trailing 1s become 0 and one 0 becomes 1):\nIncrements 1 to 16 flip 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1, 4, 1, 2, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1, 5 bits.\nTotal = 1+2+1+3+1+2+1+4+1+2+1+3+1+2+1+5 = **31 flips** (check: 2n - popcount(n) = 32 - 1 = 31).\nAmortised cost = 31/16 = 1.94 < **2 flips per increment, O(1)**, although a single increment (from 0111...1) can flip all log n bits. (Accounting argument: each 0→1 flip pays a credit that pays for the later 1→0 flip.)\n\n**Q3.**\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nstruct Node { int val; Node* next; };\n\nNode* reverse(Node* head) {                 // O(n) time, O(1) space\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    while (head) { Node* nx = head->next; head->next = prev; prev = head; head = nx; }\n    return prev;\n}\nbool hasCycle(Node* head) {                 // O(n) time, O(1) space (Floyd)\n    Node *s = head, *f = head;\n    while (f && f->next) { s = s->next; f = f->next->next; if (s == f) return true; }\n    return false;\n}\nNode* mergeSorted(Node* a, Node* b) {       // O(n + m) time, no allocation\n    Node dummy{0, nullptr}; Node* t = &dummy;\n    while (a && b) {\n        if (a->val <= b->val) { t->next = a; a = a->next; } else { t->next = b; b = b->next; }\n        t = t->next;\n    }\n    t->next = a ? a : b;\n    return dummy.next;\n}\n```\nReversing 1→2→3→4→5 gives **5 4 3 2 1**. Merging 1→4→7 with 2→3→9 gives **1 2 3 4 7 9**. For hasCycle: if the fast pointer reaches the end, there is no cycle; otherwise the fast pointer eventually catches the slow pointer inside the cycle (the gap closes by 1 each step). Using `<=` in the merge keeps equal elements from list a first, so the merge is stable. (3 marks each for reverse and merge, 2 for the cycle test, 2 for complexities.)\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Shunting-yard, tokens left to right (output | operator stack):\nStep 1: `3` → output 3.\nStep 2: `+` → stack [+].\nStep 3: `4` → output 3 4.\nStep 4: `*` binds tighter than + → stack [+ *].\nStep 5: `2` → output 3 4 2.\nStep 6: `/` has the same precedence as * and is left-associative: pop * to the output (3 4 2 *), push / → stack [+ /].\nStep 7: `(` → stack [+ / (].\nStep 8: `1` → output 3 4 2 * 1; `-` → stack [+ / ( -]; `5` → output 3 4 2 * 1 5.\nStep 9: `)` pops - to the output (3 4 2 * 1 5 -) and discards the parenthesis → stack [+ /].\nStep 10: `^` binds tighter than / (and is right-associative) → push: stack [+ / ^]; `2` → output 3 4 2 * 1 5 - 2.\nStep 11: end of input: pop ^, /, + → postfix **3 4 2 * 1 5 - 2 ^ / +**.\nEvaluate with a value stack: push 3, 4, 2; `*` → 4 × 2 = 8 (stack 3, 8); push 1, 5; `-` → 1 - 5 = -4 (3, 8, -4); push 2; `^` → (-4)^2 = 16 (3, 8, 16); `/` → 8/16 = 0.5 (3, 0.5); `+` → 3 + 0.5 = **3.5**.\n(b) Capacity 5, head = 0, size = 0:\nStep 1: enqueue 1, 2, 3, 4 write to indices (head + size) mod 5 = 0, 1, 2, 3: all succeed; array [1, 2, 3, 4, _], size 4.\nStep 2: dequeue returns **1** (head = 1, size 3); dequeue returns **2** (head = 2, size 2).\nStep 3: enqueue 5 goes to index (2 + 2) mod 5 = 4; enqueue 6 to index (2 + 3) mod 5 = 0; enqueue 7 to index (2 + 4) mod 5 = 1: size 5.\nStep 4: enqueue 8 fails: **queue full** (size = capacity).\nFinal array **[6, 7, 3, 4, 5]**, **head = 2, size = 5** (the logical order is 3, 4, 5, 6, 7).\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Array [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6].\n**Insertion sort** (comparisons / shifts per key): key 2: 1 / 1 → [2, 5, 9, 1, 5, 6]; key 9: 1 / 0; key 1: 3 / 3 → [1, 2, 5, 9, 5, 6]; key 5: 2 / 1 → [1, 2, 5, 5, 9, 6]; key 6: 2 / 1. Total **9 comparisons, 6 shifts** (O(n^2) worst case, adaptive).\n**Selection sort**: the passes make 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = **15 comparisons** (n(n - 1)/2 always). Swaps: pass 1 swaps 5 and 1; pass 2 none (2 is in place); pass 3 swaps 9 and the first 5; pass 4 swaps 9 and the second 5; pass 5 swaps 9 and 6: **4 swaps**.\n**Bubble sort with early exit**: pass 1 (5 comparisons) gives [2, 5, 1, 5, 6, 9] with 4 swaps; pass 2 (4 comparisons) gives [2, 1, 5, 5, 6, 9] with 1 swap; pass 3 (3 comparisons) gives [1, 2, 5, 5, 6, 9] with 1 swap; pass 4 (2 comparisons) makes no swap, so it stops. Total 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 = **14 comparisons and 6 swaps** (the swaps equal the number of inversions, 6). All give [1, 2, 5, 5, 6, 9].\n(b) Step 1: the pivot is 6 (last element); i = 0 marks the boundary of the elements ≤ pivot.\nStep 2: scan j = 0 to 6: 3 ≤ 6 (swap with itself, i = 1); 8 no; 2 ≤ 6 (swap a[1], a[2]: [3, 2, 8, 5, 1, 4, 7, 6], i = 2); 5 ≤ 6 (swap a[2], a[3]: [3, 2, 5, 8, 1, 4, 7, 6], i = 3); 1 ≤ 6 (swap a[3], a[4]: [3, 2, 5, 1, 8, 4, 7, 6], i = 4); 4 ≤ 6 (swap a[4], a[5]: [3, 2, 5, 1, 4, 8, 7, 6], i = 5); 7 no.\nStep 3: put the pivot into place by swapping a[5] and a[7]: **[3, 2, 5, 1, 4, 6, 7, 8]** with the pivot at index 5.\nStep 4: the complete sort needs **5 partition calls** on ranges of two or more elements (the whole array, the left part [3, 2, 5, 1, 4], the right part [7, 8] and two more inside the left part).\n(c) Build a max-heap from [4, 10, 3, 5, 1] (sift down from index n/2 - 1 = 1 to 0):\nStep 1: index 1 (key 10) has children 5 and 1: it stays.\nStep 2: index 0 (key 4) has children 10 and 3: swap with 10 → [10, 4, 3, 5, 1].\nStep 3: key 4 at index 1 has children 5 and 1: swap with 5 → the max-heap is **[10, 5, 3, 4, 1]**.\n(d) Step 1: keys 0..8; counting occurrences in [4, 2, 2, 8, 3, 3, 1] gives **[0, 1, 2, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1]** (one 1, two 2s, two 3s, one 4, one 8).\nStep 2: cumulative sums **[0, 1, 3, 5, 6, 6, 6, 6, 7]**: the number of keys ≤ each value.\nStep 3: scan the input from the right and place each key at position cumulative[key] - 1, then decrement: 1 goes to 0; the last 3 to 4; the first 3 to 3; 8 to 6; the last 2 to 2; the first 2 to 1; 4 to 5. Output [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 8]; scanning from the right keeps the sort stable.\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) Binary search halves the range each probe: at most ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 probes.\nStep 1: log2 10^6 = 19.93, so ⌊19.93⌋ + 1 = **20 probes** (2^19 = 524,288 ≤ 10^6 < 2^20 = 1,048,576).\n(b) Step 1: the decision tree needs at least 8! = 40,320 leaves (one per ordering).\nStep 2: height ≥ ⌈log2 40,320⌉ = ⌈15.30⌉ = **16 comparisons**.\n(c) A comparison sort's decision tree must distinguish n! orderings, hence height Ω(log n!) = Ω(n log n). Counting sort does not compare keys: it uses the key as an array index (it assumes small integer keys in 0..k), so the lower bound does not apply, giving Θ(n + k).\n\n**Q7.**\n```cpp\n#include <vector>\nlong long comparisons = 0;\nvoid mergeSort(std::vector<int>& a, std::vector<int>& tmp, int lo, int hi) {   // sorts a[lo, hi)\n    if (hi - lo <= 1) return;\n    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;\n    mergeSort(a, tmp, lo, mid);\n    mergeSort(a, tmp, mid, hi);\n    int i = lo, j = mid, k = lo;\n    while (i < mid && j < hi) {\n        ++comparisons;\n        if (a[i] <= a[j]) tmp[k++] = a[i++];    // <= keeps the sort stable\n        else              tmp[k++] = a[j++];\n    }\n    while (i < mid) tmp[k++] = a[i++];\n    while (j < hi)  tmp[k++] = a[j++];\n    for (k = lo; k < hi; ++k) a[k] = tmp[k];\n}\n```\nRecurrence: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + Θ(n), so **Θ(n log n)** time and Θ(n) extra space.\nCount for [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]:\nStep 1: split into [5, 2, 9] and [1, 5, 6].\nStep 2: sort [5, 2, 9]: merge [2] and [9] needs 1 comparison; merge [5] with [2, 9]: 5 vs 2 (take 2), 5 vs 9 (take 5), left exhausted: 2 comparisons. Subtotal 3.\nStep 3: sort [1, 5, 6]: merge [5] and [6]: 1 comparison; merge [1] with [5, 6]: 1 vs 5 (take 1), left exhausted: 1 comparison. Subtotal 2.\nStep 4: final merge of [2, 5, 9] and [1, 5, 6]: 2 vs 1 (take 1), 2 vs 5 (take 2), 5 vs 5 (take the left 5, which keeps the sort stable), 9 vs 5 (take 5), 9 vs 6 (take 6), right exhausted: 5 comparisons.\nStep 5: total 3 + 2 + 5 = **10 comparisons**.\nFor the sorted array 1..8: merges of single elements (4 merges × 1 comparison) + merges of 2 and 2 (2 × 2) + the merge of 4 and 4 (4 comparisons: every left element is smaller, so only the left elements are compared) = 4 + 4 + 4 = **12 comparisons** (each merge of already ordered halves needs only min(|left|, |right|)); the reverse-sorted array 8..1 also gives 12 in this implementation.\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 2: Hashing, Trees and Heaps (70 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Hash tables (12 marks)** Insert the keys 50, 700, 76, 85, 92, 73, 101 in this order into a table of size m = 7 with h(k) = k mod 7. Show the final table and the number of probes for each insertion using (a) separate chaining (give the chains) [3], (b) linear probing [4], (c) double hashing with h2(k) = 5 - (k mod 5) [4]. (d) Give the load factor [1].\n\n**Q2. Binary search trees (14 marks)** Insert 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80, 35, 45 into an empty BST. (a) Give the inorder, preorder, postorder and level-order traversals and the height [5]. (b) How many key comparisons does searching for 45 need? [2] (c) Delete 30 (two children) and give the new preorder traversal [3]. (d) What is the worst-case height of a BST with 9 keys and what input order causes it? [2] (e) How many different BST shapes exist for n = 3 and n = 4? [2]\n\n**Q3. AVL trees (10 marks)** Insert 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 25 into an empty AVL tree. Show the tree (preorder) after each insertion and name each rotation.\n\n**Q4. Heaps (10 marks)** (a) Insert 9, 4, 7, 1, 6, 2 one at a time into a min-heap; give the array after each insertion [3]. (b) Perform one extract-min and show the array [2]. (c) Give the array produced by build-heap (Floyd) on [9, 4, 7, 1, 6, 2] [2]. (d) State the complexities of insert, extract-min, peek and build-heap [1]. (e) How would you find the 3 largest of a million numbers in one pass? [2]\n\n**Q5. Huffman coding (8 marks)** For frequencies a 45, b 13, c 12, d 16, e 9, f 5 build the Huffman tree, give the code lengths and the total encoded size for 100 symbols; compare with a fixed-length code.\n\n**Q6. Union-find (6 marks)** Elements 0 to 7 start in separate sets. Perform union(0,1), union(2,3), union(1,3), union(4,5), union(6,7), union(5,7), union(3,7). Show the number of sets after each operation and explain path compression and union by rank.\n\n**Q7. Trees and code (10 marks)** (a) Reconstruct the binary tree with preorder [3, 9, 20, 15, 7] and inorder [9, 3, 15, 20, 7] and give its postorder [4]. (b) Write BST insert, contains and inorder functions in C++ and show the output of inserting 5, 3, 8, 1, 4 [4]. (c) Why does inserting 1, 2, 3, ..., n in order produce a poor BST, and how do AVL trees fix it? [2]\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\nStep 0: h(k) = k mod 7: 50 → 1, 700 → 0 (7 × 100), 76 → 6, 85 → 1, 92 → 1, 73 → 3, 101 → 3.\n(a) **Chaining:** slot 0: [700]; slot 1: [50, 85, 92]; slot 2: []; slot 3: [73, 101]; slot 4: []; slot 5: []; slot 6: [76]. Inserting at the end of a chain (kept in insertion order) costs 1 probe for the first key in a slot, 2 for the second and 3 for the third: probes 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 2 (total 11).\n(b) **Linear probing** (try h, h + 1, h + 2, ... mod 7):\n50 → slot 1 (1 probe); 700 → slot 0 (1); 76 → slot 6 (1);\n85: slot 1 is full, slot 2 is free (2 probes);\n92: slots 1 and 2 are full, slot 3 is free (3 probes);\n73: slot 3 is full, slot 4 is free (2 probes);\n101: slots 3 and 4 are full, slot 5 is free (3 probes).\n**Table [700, 50, 85, 92, 73, 101, 76]**; probes 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 2, 3 (total 13). Note the clustering in slots 1 to 5.\n(c) **Double hashing**, h2(k) = 5 - (k mod 5), probe sequence (h1 + j·h2) mod 7:\n50 → slot 1; 700 → slot 0; 76 → slot 6 (1 probe each).\n85: h1 = 1, h2 = 5 - 0 = 5: slots 1 (full), 6 (full), 4 (free): 3 probes.\n92: h1 = 1, h2 = 5 - 2 = 3: slots 1 (full), 4 (full), 0 (full), 3 (free): 4 probes.\n73: h1 = 3 (full), h2 = 5 - 3 = 2: slots 3, 5 (free): 2 probes.\n101: h1 = 3, h2 = 5 - 1 = 4: slots 3, 0, 4, 1, 5 (all full), 2 (free): 6 probes.\n**Table [700, 50, 101, 92, 85, 73, 76]**; probes 1, 1, 1, 3, 4, 2, 6 (total 18). The long probe sequence for 101 shows that a table this full (α = 1) is a poor choice for open addressing; because 7 is prime every step size is coprime with it, so every slot is eventually visited.\n(d) α = n/m = 7/7 = **1.0**. For open addressing the table is full; practical tables keep α ≤ 0.5 to 0.7 and resize.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) The tree: 50 has children 30 and 70; 30 has children 20 and 40; 40 has children 35 and 45; 70 has children 60 and 80.\nInorder (left, node, right): **20 30 35 40 45 50 60 70 80** (sorted). Preorder (node, left, right): **50 30 20 40 35 45 70 60 80**. Postorder (left, right, node): **20 35 45 40 30 60 80 70 50**. Level order: **50 30 70 20 40 60 80 35 45**. Height (edges on the longest path 50-30-40-35): **3**.\n(b) 45 is found after comparing with 50, 30, 40 and 45: **4 comparisons**.\n(c) Step 1: 30 has two children; its inorder successor is the minimum of the right subtree: 35 (the left-most node under 40).\nStep 2: replace the key 30 by 35 and delete the leaf 35 from the right subtree (40 keeps only its right child 45).\nStep 3: the tree is now 50 (35 (20, 40 (-, 45)), 70 (60, 80)), with preorder **50 35 20 40 45 70 60 80**.\n(d) The worst-case height is **8** (a chain), caused by inserting the keys in sorted (or reverse-sorted) order; every operation is then Θ(n).\n(e) Step 1: the number of BST shapes with n nodes is the Catalan number C_n = (1/(n + 1)) C(2n, n).\nStep 2: n = 3: (1/4) × C(6, 3) = 20/4 = **5**. n = 4: (1/5) × C(8, 4) = 70/5 = **14**.\n\n**Q3.** Preorder after each insertion:\n- Insert 10: **[10]**.\n- Insert 20: **[10, 20]**.\n- Insert 30: the node 10 is right-heavy (an RR case): **left rotation at 10** gives **[20, 10, 30]**.\n- Insert 40: **[20, 10, 30, 40]** (no imbalance).\n- Insert 50: node 30 becomes right-heavy (RR): **left rotation at 30** gives **[20, 10, 40, 30, 50]**.\n- Insert 25: it goes to the left of 30; node 20 is now unbalanced (its right subtree has height 3, the left 1) in the right-left (RL) case: **right rotation at 40's subtree, then left rotation at 20** (a double rotation) gives **[30, 20, 10, 25, 40, 50]** with root 30.\nThe final tree has height 2 for 6 keys (balanced).\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) Min-heap insertion (append, then sift up while smaller than the parent):\nInsert 9: [9].\nInsert 4: appended after 9, smaller than its parent, swap: [4, 9].\nInsert 7: parent 4 is smaller, no swap: [4, 9, 7].\nInsert 1: appended at index 3, parent 9: swap; then parent 4: swap: [1, 4, 7, 9].\nInsert 6: index 4, parent 4 is smaller: [1, 4, 7, 9, 6].\nInsert 2: index 5, parent 7: swap; then parent 1 is smaller: **[1, 4, 2, 9, 6, 7]**.\n(b) Extract-min returns 1; move the last element 7 to the root: [7, 4, 2, 9, 6]; sift down: the smaller child is 2, swap: **[2, 4, 7, 9, 6]**.\n(c) Floyd's build-heap on [9, 4, 7, 1, 6, 2] (sift down for i = 2, 1, 0):\nStep 1: i = 2 (key 7) has the child 2 at index 5: swap → [9, 4, 2, 1, 6, 7].\nStep 2: i = 1 (key 4) has children 1 and 6: swap with 1 → [9, 1, 2, 4, 6, 7].\nStep 3: i = 0 (key 9) has children 1 and 2: swap with 1 → [1, 9, 2, 4, 6, 7]; then 9 at index 1 has children 4 and 6: swap with 4.\nResult **[1, 4, 2, 9, 6, 7]**.\n(d) insert **O(log n)**; extract-min **O(log n)**; peek **O(1)**; build-heap **O(n)**.\n(e) Keep a **min-heap of size 3**: for each new number, if it is larger than the heap's minimum, replace the minimum and sift down. Time O(n log 3) = O(n), memory O(3).\n\nStep 1: merge the two smallest weights f(5) + e(9) = 14.\nStep 2: the smallest now are c(12) and b(13): c + b = 25.\nStep 3: the smallest are 14 and d(16): 14 + 16 = 30.\nStep 4: the smallest are 25 and 30: 25 + 30 = 55.\nStep 5: a(45) + 55 = 100 (the root).\nCode lengths (depth in the tree): **a = 1, b = 3, c = 3, d = 3, e = 4, f = 4** (for example a = 0, c = 100, b = 101, f = 1100, e = 1101, d = 111).\nTotal bits for 100 symbols: 45(1) + 13(3) + 12(3) + 16(3) + 9(4) + 5(4) = 45 + 39 + 36 + 48 + 36 + 20 = **224 bits**, versus 100 × 3 = **300 bits** for a fixed 3-bit code (6 symbols need ⌈log2 6⌉ = 3 bits): a saving of 76/300 = **25 percent**. The code is prefix-free and optimal.\n\n**Q6.** After each union the number of sets is: 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, **1**: after union(0,1): {0,1}; union(2,3): {2,3}; union(1,3): {0,1,2,3}; union(4,5): {4,5}; union(6,7): {6,7}; union(5,7): {4,5,6,7}; union(3,7): all eight. **Path compression**: during find, every node on the path is made to point directly to the root, flattening the tree. **Union by rank (or size)**: attach the shorter tree under the taller one so the tree height stays O(log n). Together they give an amortised cost of O(α(n)) per operation (inverse Ackermann, ≤ 5 for any practical n).\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) The first preorder element 3 is the root. In the inorder sequence, 9 is to the left of 3 and 15, 20, 7 are to the right. Left subtree: preorder [9], inorder [9]. Right subtree: preorder [20, 15, 7], inorder [15, 20, 7]: root 20 with left 15 and right 7. The tree: 3 (left 9, right 20 (left 15, right 7)). **Postorder: 9 15 7 20 3.**\n(b)\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\nstruct TNode { int key; TNode *l = nullptr, *r = nullptr; explicit TNode(int k) : key(k) {} };\nTNode* insert(TNode* t, int k) {\n    if (!t) return new TNode(k);\n    if (k < t->key) t->l = insert(t->l, k); else t->r = insert(t->r, k);\n    return t;\n}\nbool contains(const TNode* t, int k) {\n    while (t) { if (k == t->key) return true; t = (k < t->key) ? t->l : t->r; }\n    return false;\n}\nvoid inorder(const TNode* t, std::vector<int>& out) {\n    if (!t) return;\n    inorder(t->l, out); out.push_back(t->key); inorder(t->r, out);\n}\n```\nInserting 5, 3, 8, 1, 4: inorder gives **1 3 4 5 8**; contains(4) is true and contains(7) is false.\n(c) Sorted insertion makes each new key the right child of the previous one: the tree is a linked list of height n - 1, so search, insert and delete cost Θ(n). An AVL tree restores balance with rotations after each insertion (height ≤ 1.44 log n), keeping all operations Θ(log n).\n\n---\n\n# Assignment 3: Graphs, Dynamic Programming, Greedy and Backtracking (80 marks)\n\n## Questions\n\n**Q1. Graph traversal (12 marks)** A directed graph has edges 0→1, 0→2, 1→3, 2→3, 2→4, 3→5, 4→5. (a) Give the BFS visiting order from 0 and the distance of each vertex (visit neighbours in increasing order) [4]. (b) Give the DFS preorder [3]. (c) Give a topological order using Kahn's algorithm [3]. (d) Explain how to detect a cycle [2].\n\n**Q2. Shortest paths (16 marks)** (a) Run Dijkstra from S on edges S→A 4, S→B 1, B→A 2, A→C 1, B→C 5, B→D 8, C→D 3; give the distances and the shortest path to D [6]. (b) Bellman-Ford on s→a 4, s→b 5, a→b -3, b→c 4, a→c 6: give the distances from s [4]. (c) Explain why Dijkstra can fail with negative edges [2]. (d) Floyd-Warshall on the 4-vertex matrix with rows (0, 3, ∞, 7), (8, 0, 2, ∞), (5, ∞, 0, 1), (2, ∞, ∞, 0): give the final matrix [4].\n\n**Q3. Minimum spanning tree (10 marks)** Edges: AB 7, AD 5, BC 8, BD 9, BE 7, CE 5, DE 15, DF 6, EF 8, EG 9, FG 11. Find the MST with Kruskal's algorithm (list the edges in the order they are accepted) and with Prim's algorithm from A; give the total weight and the number of edges.\n\n**Q4. Dynamic programming (20 marks)** (a) 0/1 knapsack with weights {1, 3, 4, 5}, values {1, 4, 5, 7}, capacity 7: build the table and give the best value and the chosen items [6]. (b) LCS of \"ABCBDAB\" and \"BDCABA\": length and one subsequence [4]. (c) Edit distance between \"kitten\" and \"sitting\" with the operations [4]. (d) Minimum coins for 11 with coins {1, 5, 6, 9} [3]. (e) Longest increasing subsequence of [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18] [3].\n\n**Q5. Greedy algorithms (8 marks)** (a) Activity selection on (1,4), (3,5), (0,6), (5,7), (3,9), (5,9), (6,10), (8,11), (8,12), (2,14), (12,16): which activities does the greedy rule select and why is it optimal? [4] (b) Show that the greedy coin strategy fails for {1, 5, 6, 9} and amount 11 [2]. (c) Fractional knapsack: items (weight, value) (10, 60), (20, 100), (30, 120), capacity 50: greedy value? [2]\n\n**Q6. Backtracking and strings (8 marks)** (a) Give the number of solutions of the N-Queens problem for n = 4, 6, 8 and explain pruning [4]. (b) Compute the KMP failure array of \"ABABCABAB\" and use it to explain how matching avoids re-scanning [4].\n\n**Q7. Intractability (6 marks)** (a) Define P, NP and NP-complete [3]. (b) How many tours does brute-force TSP examine for 10 cities, and what does dynamic programming achieve? [3]\n\n## Solutions and Marking Guide\n\n**Q1.**\n(a) BFS from 0 (queue; neighbours in increasing order):\nStep 1: queue [0]; visit 0 (distance 0), enqueue 1 and 2 (distance 1).\nStep 2: visit 1: enqueue 3 (distance 2). Visit 2: 3 already seen; enqueue 4 (distance 2).\nStep 3: visit 3: enqueue 5 (distance 3). Visit 4: 5 already seen. Visit 5.\nOrder **0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5**. Distances: 0: 0; 1: 1; 2: 1; 3: 2; 4: 2; 5: 3.\n(b) DFS preorder (neighbours in increasing order): 0 → 1 → 3 → 5 (dead end, backtrack to 0) → 2 → 4 (3 and 5 already visited): **0, 1, 3, 5, 2, 4**.\n(c) Step 1: in-degrees: 0:0, 1:1, 2:1, 3:2, 4:1, 5:2.\nStep 2: queue [0]. Remove 0: in-degrees of 1 and 2 become 0 → queue [1, 2].\nStep 3: remove 1: 3 drops to 1. Remove 2: 3 drops to 0 and 4 to 0 → queue [3, 4].\nStep 4: remove 3: 5 drops to 1. Remove 4: 5 drops to 0. Remove 5.\nOrder **0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5** (a valid topological order; others exist, such as 0, 2, 1, 4, 3, 5).\n(d) In a directed graph, a **cycle exists iff DFS finds a back edge** (an edge to a vertex that is on the current recursion stack, coloured \"grey\"), or iff Kahn's algorithm cannot output all vertices. In an undirected graph, an edge to a visited vertex that is not the parent signals a cycle.\n\n**Q2.**\n(a) Dijkstra from S (settle the unsettled vertex with the smallest distance, relax its edges):\nStep 1: S = 0; relax S→A: A = 4, S→B: B = 1.\nStep 2: settle B (1): A = min(4, 1 + 2) = 3, C = 1 + 5 = 6, D = 1 + 8 = 9.\nStep 3: settle A (3): C = min(6, 3 + 1) = 4.\nStep 4: settle C (4): D = min(9, 4 + 3) = 7.\nStep 5: settle D (7).\nFinal distances **S 0, B 1, A 3, C 4, D 7**. The shortest path to D is **S → B → A → C → D** (length 1 + 2 + 1 + 3 = 7).\n(b) Bellman-Ford: relax all edges (s→a 4, s→b 5, a→b -3, b→c 4, a→c 6) up to V - 1 = 3 times.\nPass 1: a = 4; b = min(5, 4 - 3) = 1; c = min(1 + 4, 4 + 6) = 5. Pass 2: no change, so the algorithm can stop. **Distances: a 4, b 1, c 5.** A further pass changes nothing, so there is no negative cycle.\n(c) Dijkstra assumes that once a vertex is removed from the priority queue its distance is final, which requires that extending a path can never make it shorter; with a negative edge a later path through a finalised vertex could be shorter, so the result can be wrong. Use Bellman-Ford instead.\n(d) Floyd-Warshall: D[i][j] = min(D[i][j], D[i][k] + D[k][j]) for k = 0, 1, 2, 3.\nAfter k = 0: row 1 improves 1→3 to 8 + 7 = 15; row 3 improves 3→1 to 2 + 3 = 5; row 2 improves 2→1 to 5 + 3 = 8: rows (0, 3, ∞, 7), (8, 0, 2, 15), (5, 8, 0, 1), (2, 5, ∞, 0).\nAfter k = 1: 0→2 becomes 3 + 2 = 5; 3→2 becomes 5 + 2 = 7: rows (0, 3, 5, 7), (8, 0, 2, 15), (5, 8, 0, 1), (2, 5, 7, 0).\nAfter k = 2: 0→3 becomes 5 + 1 = 6; 1→0 becomes 2 + 5 = 7; 1→3 becomes 2 + 1 = 3: rows (0, 3, 5, 6), (7, 0, 2, 3), (5, 8, 0, 1), (2, 5, 7, 0).\nAfter k = 3: 1→0 becomes 3 + 2 = 5; 2→0 becomes 1 + 2 = 3; 2→1 becomes 1 + 5 = 6.\nFinal matrix **(0, 3, 5, 6), (5, 0, 2, 3), (3, 6, 0, 1), (2, 5, 7, 0)**. For example, the distance from vertex 1 to vertex 0 improves from the direct edge 8 to 5 along 1→2→3→0 (2 + 1 + 2).\n\n**Kruskal:** Step 1: sort the edges: AD 5, CE 5, DF 6, AB 7, BE 7, BC 8, EF 8, BD 9, EG 9, FG 11, DE 15.\nStep 2: accept AD (5), CE (5), DF (6), AB (7), BE (7) (each joins two different components).\nStep 3: reject BC (it would close the cycle B-C-E), EF (cycle E-B-A-D-F) and BD (cycle).\nStep 4: accept EG (9), which connects G. That is 6 edges for 7 vertices, **total weight 5 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 7 + 9 = 39**.\n**Prim from A:** grow the tree by the cheapest edge leaving it: AD (5); then DF (6); then AB (7); then BE (7); then CE (5); then EG (9): the same tree with **weight 39** (order of vertices added: A, D, F, B, E, C, G). (4 marks for each algorithm, 2 for weight and edge count.)\n\n**Q4.**\n(a) dp[i][c] = max(dp[i-1][c], v_i + dp[i-1][c - w_i]) for items in order (w, v) = (1, 1), (3, 4), (4, 5), (5, 7).\nExample cells: dp[2][3] = max(dp[1][3] = 1, 4 + dp[1][0] = 4) = 4; dp[3][7] = max(dp[2][7] = 5, 5 + dp[2][3] = 9) = 9; dp[4][7] = max(dp[3][7] = 9, 7 + dp[3][2] = 8) = 9.\nc: 0 1 2 3 4 5 6 7\ni = 0: 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0\ni = 1: 0 1 1 1 1 1 1 1\ni = 2: 0 1 1 4 5 5 5 5\ni = 3: 0 1 1 4 5 6 6 9\ni = 4: 0 1 1 4 5 7 8 9\nThe best value is **dp[4][7] = 9**. Backtrack: dp[4][7] = dp[3][7], so item 4 is not taken; dp[3][7] ≠ dp[2][7], so item 3 (weight 4) is taken, leaving capacity 3; dp[2][3] ≠ dp[1][3], so item 2 (weight 3) is taken, leaving capacity 0. **Items: weights 3 and 4, values 4 + 5 = 9.** Time Θ(nW).\n(b) Recurrence: if the characters match, L[i][j] = L[i-1][j-1] + 1, else max(L[i-1][j], L[i][j-1]). Table for X = ABCBDAB (rows) and Y = BDCABA (columns B, D, C, A, B, A):\nrow A: 0 0 0 1 1 1\nrow B: 1 1 1 1 2 2\nrow C: 1 1 2 2 2 2\nrow B: 1 1 2 2 3 3\nrow D: 1 2 2 2 3 3\nrow A: 1 2 2 3 3 4\nrow B: 1 2 2 3 4 4\n**LCS length 4** (the bottom-right cell). Backtracking from it gives for example **BCBA** (also BDAB).\n(c) D[i][j] = min(D[i-1][j] + 1, D[i][j-1] + 1, D[i-1][j-1] + [a_i ≠ b_j]), with D[i][0] = i and D[0][j] = j. The last row of the table for \"kitten\" against \"sitting\" is 6, 6, 5, 4, 3, 3, 2, 3, so the **edit distance is 3**: kitten → sitten (substitute k→s) → sittin (substitute e→i) → sitting (insert g).\n(d) dp[x] = 1 + min over coins c ≤ x of dp[x - c]. Values for x = 0..11: 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 2 (for example dp[11] = 1 + min(dp[10], dp[6], dp[5], dp[2]) = 1 + min(2, 1, 1, 2) = 2). **dp[11] = 2** (5 + 6). The greedy answer is 3.\n(e) L[i] = 1 + max L[j] over j < i with a[j] < a[i]. For [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18] the lengths of the longest increasing subsequence ending at each element are [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 4]. **LIS length 4** (for example 2, 3, 7, 18 or 2, 5, 7, 101).\n\n**Q5.**\n(a) Step 1: sort by finish time: (1,4), (3,5), (0,6), (5,7), (3,9), (5,9), (6,10), (8,11), (8,12), (2,14), (12,16).\nStep 2: take (1,4); skip (3,5) and (0,6) (they start before 4); take (5,7); skip (3,9), (5,9), (6,10); take (8,11); skip (8,12), (2,14); take (12,16).\nSelected: **(1,4), (5,7), (8,11), (12,16)**: 4 activities. **Why optimal (exchange argument):** any optimal solution can be changed to start with the earliest-finishing activity without reducing its size, because that activity leaves the most room for the rest; apply the argument repeatedly.\n(b) For 11 with coins {1, 5, 6, 9}: greedy takes 9 (remaining 2), then 1, then 1: **3 coins**; the optimum is 5 + 6 = **2 coins**. The greedy choice property fails for this coin system (it holds for canonical systems such as {1, 5, 10, 25}).\n(c) Step 1: value/weight ratios: 60/10 = 6, 100/20 = 5, 120/30 = 4.\nStep 2: take all of items 1 and 2 (weight 30, value 160); the remaining capacity is 20, so take 20/30 of item 3 (value 120 × 2/3 = 80).\nStep 3: **total value 160 + 80 = 240**. (The 0/1 version would give 220.)\n\n**Q6.**\n(a) The numbers of solutions are **n = 4: 2, n = 6: 4, n = 8: 92**. Backtracking places queens row by row and **prunes** a branch as soon as a queen attacks an earlier one (same column or diagonal), so it never explores the full n^n placements; for n = 8 it examines only a few thousand partial boards instead of 8^8 = 16.7 million.\n(b) Step 1: failure[i] = the length of the longest proper prefix of the pattern that is also a suffix of the pattern prefix ending at position i. For \"ABABCABAB\": A: 0; AB: 0; ABA: 1 (A); ABAB: 2 (AB); ABABC: 0; ABABCA: 1 (A); ABABCAB: 2 (AB); ABABCABA: 3 (ABA); ABABCABAB: 4 (ABAB).\nStep 2: the failure array is **[0, 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4]**.\nStep 3: on a mismatch after matching k characters, the pattern is shifted so that the next comparison resumes at position failure[k - 1] of the pattern instead of restarting: the text pointer never moves backwards, giving Θ(n + m) time.\n\n**Q7.**\n(a) **P:** decision problems solvable in polynomial time. **NP:** problems for which a proposed solution can be **verified** in polynomial time. **NP-complete:** problems in NP to which every problem in NP can be reduced in polynomial time (SAT, subset sum, Hamiltonian cycle, graph colouring, TSP decision version). Whether P = NP is open.\n(b) Step 1: brute force fixes the start city and tries all orderings of the other 9 cities: 9! = **362,880 tours** (10! = 3,628,800 if the start is not fixed; halve the count if direction is ignored).\nStep 2: the **Held-Karp dynamic programme** computes the optimal tour in **O(n^2 2^n)** time by storing the best path over each subset of cities: for n = 10 that is about 10^2 × 2^10 ≈ 10^5 operations, still exponential but far better than n!.\n","exam":"# Data Structures and Algorithms: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. No computer or notes. Total: 100 marks. Code is C++17 pseudocode-level: syntax errors will not be penalised if the intention is clear.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Two nested loops, each running n times, give a running time of\nA) Θ(n)  B) Θ(n log n)  C) Θ(n^2)  D) Θ(2^n)\n\n**2.** Building a binary heap from n arbitrary elements with Floyd's method takes\nA) Θ(n)  B) Θ(n log n)  C) Θ(n^2)  D) Θ(log n)\n\n**3.** The maximum number of probes of binary search on 1000 sorted items is about\nA) 3  B) 10  C) 100  D) 1000\n\n**4.** The load factor of a hash table with 50 slots holding 100 keys (chaining) is\nA) 0.5  B) 1  C) 2  D) 50\n\n**5.** The height of an AVL tree with n nodes is\nA) Θ(1)  B) Θ(log n)  C) Θ(n)  D) Θ(n log n)\n\n**6.** In an unweighted graph, the shortest path (fewest edges) from a source is found by\nA) DFS  B) BFS  C) Kruskal  D) topological sort\n\n**7.** Kruskal's algorithm detects cycles with\nA) a queue  B) union-find  C) a stack  D) binary search\n\n**8.** The recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n solves to\nA) Θ(n)  B) Θ(n log n)  C) Θ(n^2)  D) Θ(log n)\n\n**9.** Dynamic programming differs from divide and conquer in that its subproblems\nA) are independent  B) overlap  C) are always smaller by half  D) are unsolvable\n\n**10.** The inorder traversal of a BST produces\nA) sorted order  B) reverse order  C) level order  D) random order\n\n**11.** Counting sort of n integers in the range 0..k runs in\nA) Θ(n log n)  B) Θ(n + k)  C) Θ(n k)  D) Θ(k log n)\n\n**12.** Which of these is a stable sort?\nA) heapsort  B) merge sort  C) selection sort (swap-based)  D) quicksort (typical)\n\n**13.** A topological sort is possible for\nA) all graphs  B) directed acyclic graphs  C) undirected graphs  D) complete graphs\n\n**14.** A circular array queue uses indices computed\nA) modulo the capacity  B) by sorting  C) randomly  D) by hashing\n\n**15.** Quicksort with the last element as pivot is worst on\nA) random data  B) already sorted data  C) data with many ties only  D) very short arrays\n\n**16.** Dijkstra's algorithm can fail when the graph has\nA) cycles  B) negative edge weights  C) multiple edges  D) many vertices\n\n**17.** Which of these is NP-complete?\nA) sorting  B) Boolean satisfiability (SAT)  C) binary search  D) finding the minimum of an array\n\n**18.** The memory used by a recursive DFS on a tree of height h (excluding the tree itself) is\nA) O(1)  B) O(h)  C) O(n^2)  D) O(2^h)\n\n**19.** The amortised cost of vector::push_back is\nA) O(n)  B) O(log n)  C) O(1)  D) O(n^2)\n\n**20.** A Huffman code is\nA) fixed-length  B) prefix-free and optimal for the given frequencies  C) always 8 bits per symbol  D) not decodable\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** Give the Θ-class and justify: `for (i = 0; i < n; i++) { for (j = 0; j < n; j += 2) work(); } for (k = 1; k < n; k *= 2) work();`\n\n**B2. (5)** Convert `5 + ( 1 + 2 ) * 4 - 3` to postfix and evaluate it with a stack. (Hint: the postfix is `5 1 2 + 4 * + 3 -`.) Show the stack after each token.\n\n**B3. (5)** Build a max-heap from [3, 1, 6, 5, 2, 4] by Floyd's method (sift down from index 2 to 0) and give the array, and the array after the first heapsort extraction (swap the root with the last element and sift down in the shortened heap).\n\n**B4. (5)** How many comparisons does binary search need in the worst case for n = 1000? Explain with the decision tree. How many for n = 1,000,000?\n\n**B5. (5)** A hash table with chaining holds n = 100 keys in m = 50 slots. Give the load factor, the expected number of keys examined in an unsuccessful search, and in a successful search (using 1 + α/2).\n\n**B6. (5)** For a tree with root 1, children 2 and 3, node 2 has children 4 and 5, node 3 has child 6, give the DFS preorder and the BFS order (children left to right).\n\n**B7. (5)** How many different binary search tree shapes exist with 5 nodes? Give the formula.\n\n**B8. (5)** Tasks have dependencies A → C, B → C, C → D, B → E, E → D (X → Y means X must precede Y). Use Kahn's algorithm to produce a valid order and state how to detect an impossible schedule.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. Graph algorithms.** An undirected graph on S, A, B, C, D has edges S-A 3, S-B 6, A-B 2, A-C 5, B-C 1, B-D 4, C-D 3.\n(a) Run Dijkstra from S and give the distances and the shortest path to D. [4]\n(b) Find a minimum spanning tree (Kruskal) and its weight. [3]\n(c) Is the MST unique? Explain. [1]\n(d) State the complexity of Dijkstra with a binary heap and with an array. [2]\n\n**C2. Heap application.** Merge k sorted lists with a min-heap.\n(a) Write C++ for `vector<int> mergeK(const vector<vector<int>>&)` using a priority queue of (value, list index, position). [5]\n(b) Trace the heap contents for the lists {1, 5, 9}, {2, 6, 8}, {3, 4, 7}: give the first six values extracted. [2]\n(c) State the time complexity in terms of N (total elements) and k, and why it is better than concatenating and sorting when k is small. [3]\n\n**C3. Balanced trees.**\n(a) Insert 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7 into an empty AVL tree; name each rotation and give the final tree (preorder). [6]\n(b) Compare with the BST you would get without balancing: its height. [2]\n(c) State the time for search, insert and delete in an AVL tree and the extra information stored. [2]\n\n**C4. Dynamic programming.**\n(a) Rod cutting with prices p = [1, 5, 8, 9, 10, 17, 17, 20] for lengths 1 to 8: write the recurrence and compute the best revenue for lengths 1 to 8. [5]\n(b) Compute the length of the longest common subsequence of \"AGGTAB\" and \"GXTXAYB\" and give one subsequence. [3]\n(c) State the time and space complexity of each. [2]\n\n**C5. Cache design.** Design an LRU cache with capacity 2 supporting `get(key)` and `put(key, value)` in O(1).\n(a) Name the two data structures that give O(1) for both operations and explain their roles. [3]\n(b) Trace: put(1,1), put(2,2), get(1), put(3,3), get(2), put(4,4), get(1), get(3), get(4): give the output of each get. [4]\n(c) Why does a plain hash table with a timestamp scan not meet the O(1) requirement? [1]\n(d) Name another eviction policy and its main trade-off. [2]\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **C** · 2 **A** · 3 **B** · 4 **C** · 5 **B** · 6 **B** · 7 **B** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **A** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **A** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **B** · 19 **C** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 3. ⌊log2 1000⌋ + 1 = 10. 4. 100/50. 8. Master theorem, equal case. 15. Sorted data with the last element as pivot gives a degenerate partition. 18. The recursion depth is the height.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Step 1: the first double loop runs the outer loop n times and the inner loop n/2 times (j += 2): n × n/2 = n^2/2 executions, which is Θ(n^2).\nStep 2: the second loop runs ⌈log2 n⌉ times (k doubles): Θ(log n).\nStep 3: the total is Θ(n^2) + Θ(log n) = **Θ(n^2)** (the larger term dominates).\n\n**B2.** Postfix: **5 1 2 + 4 * + 3 -**. Stack: push 5 → [5]; push 1 → [5, 1]; push 2 → [5, 1, 2]; + → [5, 3]; push 4 → [5, 3, 4]; * → [5, 12]; + → [17]; push 3 → [17, 3]; - → **[14]**. The value is **14**.\n\n**B3.** Array [3, 1, 6, 5, 2, 4], n = 6; sift down from index n/2 - 1 = 2 to 0.\nStep 1: index 2 (key 6) has the child 4 (index 5): 6 is larger, no change.\nStep 2: index 1 (key 1) has children 5 and 2: swap with 5 → [3, 5, 6, 1, 2, 4].\nStep 3: index 0 (key 3) has children 5 and 6: swap with 6 → [6, 5, 3, 1, 2, 4]; then 3 (index 2) has the child 4: swap → **[6, 5, 4, 1, 2, 3]**.\nHeapsort first extraction:\nStep 4: swap the root with the last element: [3, 5, 4, 1, 2 | 6].\nStep 5: sift down 3 in the heap of size 5: children 5 and 4, swap with 5 → **[5, 3, 4, 1, 2, 6]** (6 is in its final place).\n\n**B4.** Each comparison halves the range, so the decision tree has height ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1.\nStep 1: n = 1000: log2 1000 = 9.97, so ⌊9.97⌋ + 1 = **10 comparisons** (2^9 = 512 < 1000 ≤ 2^10 = 1024).\nStep 2: n = 1,000,000: log2 10^6 = 19.93, so ⌊19.93⌋ + 1 = **20 comparisons**.\n\n**B5.** Step 1: load factor α = n/m = 100/50 = **2**.\nStep 2: unsuccessful search: the whole chain is examined, **α = 2 keys** on average.\nStep 3: successful search: 1 + α/2 = 1 + 1 = **2 keys** on average.\nLookups stay constant-time if α is bounded; resizing keeps α below a threshold.\n\n**B6.** DFS preorder: **1, 2, 4, 5, 3, 6**. BFS order: **1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6**.\n\n**B7.** Step 1: the number of BST shapes with n nodes is the Catalan number C_n = C(2n, n)/(n + 1).\nStep 2: for n = 5: C(10, 5) = 252, and 252/6 = **42**.\n\n**B8.** In-degrees: A 0, B 0, C 2, D 2, E 1. Start with the queue {A, B}. Remove A: C's in-degree becomes 1. Remove B: C becomes 0 and E becomes 0; append C, E. Remove C: D becomes 1. Remove E: D becomes 0; append D. Remove D. **One valid order: A, B, C, E, D** (another: B, A, E, C, D). If the algorithm ends with unprocessed vertices (the queue empties while some in-degrees are still positive), the graph has a **cycle** and no valid schedule exists.\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) Dijkstra from S:\nStep 1: S = 0; relax S: A = 3, B = 6.\nStep 2: settle A (3): B = min(6, 3 + 2) = 5, C = 3 + 5 = 8.\nStep 3: settle B (5): C = min(8, 5 + 1) = 6, D = 5 + 4 = 9.\nStep 4: settle C (6): D = min(9, 6 + 3) = 9 (a tie).\nStep 5: settle D (9).\n**Distances: S 0, A 3, B 5, C 6, D 9.** Shortest paths to D: **S-A-B-D** (3 + 2 + 4 = 9) and S-A-B-C-D (3 + 2 + 1 + 3 = 9): two shortest paths of length 9.\n(b) Kruskal:\nStep 1: sort the edges: BC 1, AB 2, SA 3, CD 3, BD 4, AC 5, SB 6.\nStep 2: accept BC, AB, SA, CD (4 edges for 5 vertices); reject BD (it would form a cycle B-C-D), AC and SB.\nStep 3: **MST weight = 1 + 2 + 3 + 3 = 9.**\n(c) Yes, the MST is **unique**. By the cycle property the heaviest edge of any cycle is never needed when it is strictly heavier than the other edges of that cycle. Here the rejected edges BD (4), AC (5) and SB (6) are each the strictly heaviest edge of a cycle (B-C-D, A-B-C and S-A-B respectively), so no alternative tree of weight 9 exists.\n(d) With a binary heap: **O((V + E) log V)**; with an array (linear minimum search): **O(V^2)**, which is better for dense graphs.\n\n**C2.**\n(a)\n```cpp\n#include <queue>\n#include <tuple>\n#include <vector>\nstd::vector<int> mergeK(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& lists) {\n    using T = std::tuple<int, size_t, size_t>;                  // (value, list index, position)\n    std::priority_queue<T, std::vector<T>, std::greater<T>> pq;  // min-heap\n    for (size_t i = 0; i < lists.size(); ++i)\n        if (!lists[i].empty()) pq.emplace(lists[i][0], i, 0);\n    std::vector<int> out;\n    while (!pq.empty()) {\n        auto [v, i, p] = pq.top(); pq.pop();\n        out.push_back(v);\n        if (p + 1 < lists[i].size()) pq.emplace(lists[i][p + 1], i, p + 1);\n    }\n    return out;\n}\n```\n(b) Step 1: the heap initially holds (1, list 0), (2, list 1), (3, list 2).\nStep 2: extract **1** (push 5 from list 0); heap {2, 3, 5}. Extract **2** (push 6); heap {3, 5, 6}. Extract **3** (push 4); heap {4, 5, 6}. Extract **4** (push 7); heap {5, 6, 7}. Extract **5** (push 9); heap {6, 7, 9}. Extract **6** (push 8); heap {7, 8, 9}.\nThe first six values extracted are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 (the full result is 1 to 9 in order).\n(c) Each of the N elements is pushed and popped once from a heap of size at most k: **O(N log k)** time, **O(k)** extra memory for the heap. Concatenating and sorting costs O(N log N); for small k, log k is much smaller than log N. The method also works as a streaming merge (external sort).\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Insert 3, 2, 1: the node 3 is left-heavy (LL case): **right rotation at 3** gives root 2 with children 1 and 3. Insert 4: no rotation. Insert 5: the node 3 becomes right-heavy (RR): **left rotation at 3** gives subtree 4 (3, 5). Insert 6: the root 2 becomes right-heavy (RR): **left rotation at 2** gives root 4 with left 2 (1, 3) and right 5 (right child 6). Insert 7: the node 5 becomes right-heavy (RR): **left rotation at 5** gives 6 (5, 7). The final tree is perfect: preorder **4, 2, 1, 3, 6, 5, 7**, height 2.\n(b) Without balancing, inserting 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 7 gives a BST whose longest path is 3-4-5-6-7: **height 4** (versus 2 for the AVL tree).\n(c) Search, insert and delete are all **O(log n)**; each node stores its **height (or balance factor)**.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) Recurrence: r(0) = 0 and **r(n) = max over i = 1..n of (p_i + r(n - i))**, with p = [1, 5, 8, 9, 10, 17, 17, 20].\nStep 1: r(1) = p1 = 1.\nStep 2: r(2) = max(p2 = 5, p1 + r(1) = 2) = 5.\nStep 3: r(3) = max(p3 = 8, p1 + r(2) = 6, p2 + r(1) = 6) = 8.\nStep 4: r(4) = max(p4 = 9, p1 + r(3) = 9, p2 + r(2) = 10, p3 + r(1) = 9) = 10 (5 + 5).\nStep 5: r(5) = max(p5 = 10, 1 + r(4) = 11, 5 + r(3) = 13, 8 + r(2) = 13, 9 + r(1) = 10) = 13.\nStep 6: r(6) = p6 = 17 (the best of 17, 1 + 13 = 14, 5 + 10 = 15, 8 + 8 = 16, 9 + 5 = 14, 10 + 1 = 11).\nStep 7: r(7) = max(17, 1 + 17 = 18, 5 + 13 = 18, 8 + 10 = 18, 9 + 8 = 17, 10 + 5 = 15, 17 + 1 = 18) = 18.\nStep 8: r(8) = max(20, 1 + 18 = 19, 5 + 17 = 22, 8 + 13 = 21, 9 + 10 = 19, 10 + 8 = 18, 17 + 5 = 22, 17 + 1 = 18) = 22 (for example a piece of 2 plus a piece of 6).\nSo the revenues are **1, 5, 8, 10, 13, 17, 18, 22**.\n(b) Recurrence: if the characters match, L[i][j] = L[i-1][j-1] + 1, else max(L[i-1][j], L[i][j-1]). Table for X = AGGTAB (rows) and Y = GXTXAYB (columns G, X, T, X, A, Y, B):\nrow A: 0 0 0 0 1 1 1\nrow G: 1 1 1 1 1 1 1\nrow G: 1 1 1 1 1 1 1\nrow T: 1 1 2 2 2 2 2\nrow A: 1 1 2 2 3 3 3\nrow B: 1 1 2 2 3 3 4\nThe LCS has **length 4** (the bottom-right cell): **GTAB**.\n(c) Rod cutting: **Θ(n^2) time, Θ(n) space**. LCS: **Θ(mn) time**, Θ(mn) space (or Θ(min(m, n)) for the length only).\n\n**C5.**\n(a) A **hash map** from key to node pointer (O(1) lookup) and a **doubly linked list** ordered from most recently used (head) to least recently used (tail). On get: find the node via the map, move it to the head, return the value. On put: update and move to the head if present, otherwise insert at the head and, if the size exceeds the capacity, remove the tail node (and its map entry). All steps are O(1) since the list nodes can be unlinked in O(1) given a pointer.\n(b) Capacity 2: put(1,1): [1]; put(2,2): [2, 1]; get(1) returns **1** and the order becomes [1, 2]; put(3,3) evicts the least recently used key 2: [3, 1]; get(2) returns **-1**; put(4,4) evicts 1: [4, 3]; get(1) returns **-1**; get(3) returns **3**; get(4) returns **4**. Outputs: **1, -1, -1, 3, 4**.\n(c) Finding the least recently used entry by scanning timestamps takes O(n) per eviction. Only a structure that keeps the order incrementally (the linked list) gives O(1).\n(d) **LFU** (least frequently used) keeps frequently accessed items longer but needs frequency counters and is slower to adapt; **FIFO** is the simplest but ignores recency; **CLOCK** approximates LRU with less overhead; random eviction is cheap but unpredictable. Any one with its trade-off earns the marks.\n","notes":"# Data Structures and Algorithms: Complete Lecture Notes\n\nA **data structure** organises data so that the operations we need are efficient; an **algorithm** is a precise procedure for solving a problem. The two are inseparable: the choice of structure decides the best algorithm. Code samples are in C++17 (they are complete enough to compile with `g++ -std=c++17`), but every idea applies in any language. Notation: n is the input size, log means log base 2 unless stated, and \"O(f)\" is an upper bound.\n\n## Chapter 1. Analysing Algorithms\n\n### 1.1 Why analysis matters\nRun time depends on the machine, but the **growth** of the cost with n does not. An O(n log n) sort handles a million items in a fraction of a second; an O(n^2) one needs about an hour. We count basic operations as a function of n, in the worst case (the guarantee), the average case (expected, under assumptions about the input), or the best case (rarely useful).\n\n### 1.2 Asymptotic notation\n- **f(n) = O(g(n))**: there are constants c and n0 with f(n) ≤ c g(n) for all n ≥ n0 (upper bound). **Ω(g)**: lower bound. **Θ(g)**: both. Drop constants and lower-order terms: 3n^2 + 10n + 7 = Θ(n^2).\n- **Hierarchy:** 1 < log n < sqrt n < n < n log n < n^2 < n^3 < 2^n < n!. For n = 1,000,000: log n ≈ 20, n log n ≈ 2 × 10^7, n^2 = 10^12.\n- **Counting loops.** Two nested loops with j running to i give 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1)/2 = Θ(n^2) (136 iterations for n = 16). A loop that doubles i from 1 while i < n runs ⌈log n⌉ times (4 for n = 16). A loop of n iterations containing a halving loop is Θ(n log n).\n\n### 1.3 Recurrences\nDivide-and-conquer algorithms give recurrences: T(n) = a T(n/b) + f(n). **Master theorem** (with f(n) = Θ(n^d)): if d < log_b a then T = Θ(n^(log_b a)); if d = log_b a then Θ(n^d log n); if d > log_b a then Θ(n^d). Examples: binary search T(n) = T(n/2) + 1 gives Θ(log n); merge sort T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n gives Θ(n log n); T(n) = 4T(n/2) + n gives Θ(n^2); Karatsuba T(n) = 3T(n/2) + n gives Θ(n^1.585); T(n) = T(n - 1) + 1 gives Θ(n). The substitution method and recursion trees confirm the answers.\n\n### 1.4 Amortised analysis\nThe **amortised cost** is the average cost per operation over a worst-case sequence.\n- **Dynamic array (vector) with doubling:** pushing n items copies 1 + 2 + 4 + ... < 2n elements in total: amortised O(1) per push. For 17 pushes starting with capacity 1, the copies are 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31 < 2 × 17. (Growing by a fixed amount instead gives Θ(n^2) total.)\n- **Binary counter:** n increments flip fewer than 2n bits in total (31 flips for 16 increments from 0), so each increment is amortised O(1). Methods: aggregate, accounting (credits), potential function.\n\n## Chapter 2. Lists, Stacks and Queues\n\n### 2.1 Arrays and dynamic arrays\nContiguous memory: indexing O(1), insertion or deletion in the middle O(n), amortised O(1) append at the end, excellent cache behaviour. `std::vector` in C++, `list` in Python, `ArrayList` in Java.\n\n### 2.2 Linked lists\nNodes hold a value and pointers. A **singly linked list** has O(1) insertion at the head and at a known node, O(n) search and no random access; a **doubly linked list** adds O(1) deletion given a node and bidirectional traversal; a **circular list** and a **sentinel (dummy) head** simplify edge cases.\n```cpp\nstruct Node { int val; Node* next; };\n\nNode* reverse(Node* head) {                 // O(n) time, O(1) space\n    Node* prev = nullptr;\n    while (head) { Node* nx = head->next; head->next = prev; prev = head; head = nx; }\n    return prev;\n}\nbool hasCycle(Node* head) {                 // Floyd: slow and fast pointers\n    Node *s = head, *f = head;\n    while (f && f->next) { s = s->next; f = f->next->next; if (s == f) return true; }\n    return false;\n}\nNode* mergeSorted(Node* a, Node* b) {       // merge two sorted lists\n    Node dummy{0, nullptr}; Node* t = &dummy;\n    while (a && b) {\n        if (a->val <= b->val) { t->next = a; a = a->next; } else { t->next = b; b = b->next; }\n        t = t->next;\n    }\n    t->next = a ? a : b;\n    return dummy.next;\n}\n```\nChoose arrays unless you need frequent insertion or deletion at known positions; pointer chasing is slow on modern CPUs.\n\n### 2.3 Stacks, queues and deques\n- **Stack (LIFO):** push, pop, top, all O(1). Uses: function calls, undo, expression evaluation, matching brackets, depth-first search, backtracking. **Queue (FIFO):** enqueue, dequeue O(1) using a linked list or a **circular array** (indices modulo capacity). Uses: scheduling, buffering, breadth-first search. **Deque:** insertion and removal at both ends. **Priority queue:** see heaps.\n- **Infix to postfix (shunting-yard)** and evaluation: \"3 + 4 * 2 / ( 1 - 5 ) ^ 2\" becomes `3 4 2 * 1 5 - 2 ^ / +`, which evaluates to 3.5 with a stack (the exponent operator is right-associative). Rules: operands go to the output; an operator pops operators of higher (or equal, if left-associative) precedence from the stack first; parentheses are matched with the stack.\n- **Monotonic stack:** \"next greater element\" in O(n); **sliding window maximum** with a deque in O(n).\n\n## Chapter 3. Sorting and Searching\n\n### 3.1 Elementary sorts\nFor [5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6]: **insertion sort** (9 comparisons, 6 shifts here; O(n^2) worst case, O(n) on sorted data, stable, good for small or nearly sorted inputs); **selection sort** (always n(n - 1)/2 = 15 comparisons, at most n - 1 swaps; not stable); **bubble sort** (14 comparisons and 6 swaps with early exit; rarely used). Their cost is related to the number of **inversions** in the input.\n\n### 3.2 Efficient comparison sorts\n- **Merge sort:** split, sort halves, merge. Θ(n log n) always, stable, needs O(n) extra space. Good for linked lists and external sorting.\n- **Quicksort:** choose a pivot, partition, recurse. **Lomuto partition** with the last element as pivot: on [3, 8, 2, 5, 1, 4, 7, 6] the pivot 6 ends at index 5 giving [3, 2, 5, 1, 4, 6, 7, 8]. Average Θ(n log n), worst case Θ(n^2) (already sorted input with a bad pivot rule); fix with random or median-of-three pivots; in-place; not stable. Introsort (quicksort + heapsort fallback) is used by std::sort.\n- **Heapsort:** build a max-heap in O(n), then repeatedly move the maximum to the end: Θ(n log n) worst case, in place, not stable.\n- **Lower bound:** any comparison sort needs at least log2(n!) = Θ(n log n) comparisons (a decision tree with n! leaves has height ≥ log2(n!); for n = 8 that is 16).\n- **Non-comparison sorts:** **counting sort** (keys in 0..k: Θ(n + k), stable), **radix sort** (digit by digit with a stable sort: Θ(d(n + k))), **bucket sort** (uniform data, average Θ(n)).\n- **Stability** (equal keys keep their relative order) matters when sorting by several keys. `std::sort` is unstable; `std::stable_sort` is stable. Python's `sorted` uses Timsort (stable, adaptive).\n\n### 3.3 Searching\n- **Linear search** Θ(n). **Binary search** on sorted arrays Θ(log n): `mid = lo + (hi - lo)/2` to avoid overflow; at most ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 probes (20 for a million items). Variants: lower_bound/upper_bound, search on the answer, rotated arrays.\n- **Selection:** the k-th smallest element in expected O(n) with quickselect; median of medians gives worst-case O(n).\n\n## Chapter 4. Hashing\n\nA **hash table** maps keys to values in average O(1). A hash function h(k) maps a key to an index in a table of size m; a good function spreads keys uniformly (for integers h(k) = k mod m with m prime; for strings a polynomial rolling hash). **Load factor** α = n/m.\n- **Separate chaining:** each slot holds a list. Expected search cost 1 + α. With keys 50, 700, 76, 85, 92, 73, 101 and m = 7: slot 0 → [700]; slot 1 → [50, 85, 92]; slot 3 → [73, 101]; slot 6 → [76].\n- **Open addressing:** all entries live in the table; on a collision probe another slot. **Linear probing** h(k, i) = (h(k) + i) mod m (simple, cache-friendly, suffers from primary clustering): the same keys give the table [700, 50, 85, 92, 73, 101, 76] with 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 2, 3 probes in insertion order. **Double hashing** uses a second hash h2 (with h2(k) = 5 - (k mod 5)) for the step: it spreads the keys better but has less locality. Deletion needs tombstones. Performance degrades sharply as α → 1; resize (rehash into a table twice the size) when α exceeds a threshold, which is amortised O(1).\n- **Worst case** is O(n) (all keys collide): adversarial inputs motivate randomised or cryptographic hashing. Applications: dictionaries (`unordered_map`, Python `dict`), sets, caches, de-duplication, counting, Bloom filters, consistent hashing.\n\n## Chapter 5. Trees\n\n### 5.1 Binary trees and traversals\nA binary tree node has left and right children. Terms: root, leaf, height (edges on the longest root-to-leaf path), depth, subtree. A tree with n nodes has n - 1 edges; a perfect binary tree of height h has 2^(h+1) - 1 nodes. **Traversals:** preorder (node, left, right), inorder (left, node, right), postorder (left, right, node), and level order (BFS with a queue). Preorder plus inorder (or postorder plus inorder) uniquely determine a binary tree.\n\n### 5.2 Binary search trees\nFor every node, keys in the left subtree are smaller and keys in the right subtree are larger. Search, insert and delete are O(h): O(log n) for a balanced tree, O(n) for a degenerate one (keys inserted in sorted order). **Inorder traversal gives the keys in sorted order.** Deletion has three cases: a leaf (remove), one child (splice out), two children (replace with the inorder successor (the minimum of the right subtree) or predecessor and delete that node).\nInserting 50, 30, 70, 20, 40, 60, 80, 35, 45 gives inorder 20 30 35 40 45 50 60 70 80, preorder 50 30 20 40 35 45 70 60 80, postorder 20 35 45 40 30 60 80 70 50, level order 50 30 70 20 40 60 80 35 45, and height 3. Deleting 30 (two children) replaces it by its successor 35.\n```cpp\nstruct TNode { int key; TNode *l = nullptr, *r = nullptr; TNode(int k) : key(k) {} };\nTNode* insert(TNode* t, int k) {\n    if (!t) return new TNode(k);\n    if (k < t->key) t->l = insert(t->l, k); else t->r = insert(t->r, k);\n    return t;\n}\nbool contains(const TNode* t, int k) {\n    while (t) { if (k == t->key) return true; t = (k < t->key) ? t->l : t->r; }\n    return false;\n}\nvoid inorder(const TNode* t, std::vector<int>& out) {\n    if (!t) return; inorder(t->l, out); out.push_back(t->key); inorder(t->r, out);\n}\n```\n\n### 5.3 Balanced trees\n- **AVL trees** keep |height(left) - height(right)| ≤ 1 at every node using rotations: single right (LL case), single left (RR), left-right (LR) and right-left (RL). Height ≤ 1.44 log n, so all operations are O(log n). Inserting 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 25 gives (preorder): [10]; [10, 20]; a left rotation at 10 gives [20, 10, 30]; [20, 10, 30, 40]; a left rotation at 30 gives [20, 10, 40, 30, 50]; inserting 25 causes a right-left double rotation at 20, giving [30, 20, 10, 25, 40, 50].\n- **Red-black trees** (used in `std::map`, Java TreeMap) and **B-trees/B+ trees** (databases and file systems: many keys per node to minimise disk reads), **skip lists**, **splay trees**.\n\n### 5.4 Heaps and priority queues\nA **binary heap** is a complete binary tree stored in an array (children of i at 2i + 1 and 2i + 2) with the heap property (min-heap: parent ≤ children). **insert** (append, sift up) and **extract-min** (replace the root with the last item, sift down) are O(log n); **peek** is O(1); **build-heap** from n items by sifting down from n/2 - 1 to 0 is Θ(n). Inserting 9, 4, 7, 1, 6, 2 into a min-heap gives [1, 4, 2, 9, 6, 7]; extract-min removes 1 and leaves [2, 4, 7, 9, 6]. Build-heap of [4, 10, 3, 5, 1] as a max-heap gives [10, 5, 3, 4, 1]. Uses: priority queues, Dijkstra, event simulation, top-k (keep a heap of size k: O(n log k)), merging k sorted lists, heapsort.\n\n### 5.5 Tries and other structures\nA **trie** stores strings along root-to-node paths: insert/search in O(length) independent of the number of words; used for autocomplete and IP routing. **Union-find (disjoint sets)** supports `find` and `union` with **path compression** and **union by rank** in nearly constant amortised time (inverse Ackermann). After the unions (0,1), (2,3), (1,3), (4,5), (6,7), (5,7), (3,7) all of 0 to 7 are in one set. **Segment trees and Fenwick trees** answer range queries in O(log n). **Huffman coding** (below) uses a priority queue.\n\n## Chapter 6. Graph Algorithms\n\n### 6.1 Representation and traversal\nAdjacency list (O(V + E) space; best for sparse graphs) or matrix (O(V^2); O(1) edge test).\n- **BFS** (queue): visits vertices in order of distance from the source, finds shortest paths in unweighted graphs, O(V + E). **DFS** (stack or recursion): finds connected components, detects cycles, classifies edges, O(V + E).\n- On the DAG 0 → {1, 2}, 1 → 3, 2 → {3, 4}, 3 → 5, 4 → 5: BFS from 0 visits 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 with distances 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3; DFS visits 0, 1, 3, 5, 2, 4 (neighbours in increasing order).\n- **Topological sort** of a DAG: Kahn's algorithm (repeatedly remove vertices of in-degree 0) or the reverse DFS finishing order; the example above gives 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. A graph has a topological order iff it has no cycle. Uses: build systems, course prerequisites, task scheduling.\n\n### 6.2 Shortest paths\n- **Dijkstra** (non-negative weights): priority queue, O((V + E) log V). Relax edges from the closest unfinished vertex. For S→A 4, S→B 1, B→A 2, A→C 1, B→C 5, B→D 8, C→D 3: distances A = 3, B = 1, C = 4, D = 7 with the path S-B-A-C-D.\n- **Bellman-Ford** (negative edges allowed, detects negative cycles): relax all edges V - 1 times, O(VE). **Floyd-Warshall** all-pairs: three nested loops, O(V^3). **A\\*** adds a heuristic.\n\n### 6.3 Minimum spanning trees\n- **Kruskal:** sort the edges, add each edge that joins two different components (union-find): O(E log E). **Prim:** grow a tree from a vertex with a priority queue: O(E log V). Both rely on the **cut property**. On the 7-vertex example with edges AB 7, AD 5, BC 8, BD 9, BE 7, CE 5, DE 15, DF 6, EF 8, EG 9, FG 11, the MST has weight 39 (edges AD 5, CE 5, DF 6, AB 7, BE 7, EG 9).\n- Other topics: strongly connected components (Kosaraju, Tarjan), bipartite checking by BFS colouring, maximum flow (Ford-Fulkerson), matching.\n\n## Chapter 7. Algorithm Design Paradigms\n\n### 7.1 Divide and conquer\nMerge sort, quicksort, binary search, closest pair, fast exponentiation (x^n in O(log n) multiplications), Strassen and Karatsuba multiplication, fast Fourier transform.\n\n### 7.2 Greedy algorithms\nMake the locally best choice and never reconsider. Correct when the problem has the **greedy-choice property** and optimal substructure; prove it by an exchange argument.\n- **Activity selection:** sort by finish time and take each compatible activity: from (1,4), (3,5), (0,6), (5,7), (3,9), (5,9), (6,10), (8,11), (8,12), (2,14), (12,16) the greedy answer is (1,4), (5,7), (8,11), (12,16), i.e. 4 activities.\n- **Fractional knapsack** (take items by value per weight), **Huffman coding** (repeatedly merge the two least frequent symbols): for frequencies a 45, b 13, c 12, d 16, e 9, f 5 the code lengths are 1, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4 and the expected cost is 2.24 bits per symbol (224 bits per 100 symbols against 300 for a fixed 3-bit code), plus Kruskal, Prim and Dijkstra.\n- **Greedy can fail:** making change for 11 with coins {1, 5, 6, 9}: greedy takes 9 + 1 + 1 = 3 coins but the optimum is 5 + 6 = 2 coins.\n\n### 7.3 Dynamic programming\nSolve overlapping subproblems once and store the results (**memoisation** top-down, or **tabulation** bottom-up). Steps: define the state, write the recurrence, set the base cases, choose the evaluation order, extract the answer (and the solution by backtracking).\n- **Fibonacci:** F(n) = F(n - 1) + F(n - 2): naive recursion is Θ(φ^n), memoised Θ(n) (177 calls for fib(10) naively).\n- **0/1 knapsack:** dp[i][c] = max(dp[i-1][c], dp[i-1][c - w_i] + v_i). Weights {1, 3, 4, 5}, values {1, 4, 5, 7}, capacity 7: the best value is 9 (items of weight 3 and 4). Θ(nW) pseudo-polynomial.\n- **Longest common subsequence:** L[i][j] = L[i-1][j-1] + 1 if the characters match, else max(L[i-1][j], L[i][j-1]). LCS(\"ABCBDAB\", \"BDCABA\") has length 4 (for example BCBA). **Edit distance** (insert, delete, substitute) between \"kitten\" and \"sitting\" is 3. **Coin change** (minimum coins): for {1, 5, 6, 9} and 11, dp gives 2. **Longest increasing subsequence** of [10, 9, 2, 5, 3, 7, 101, 18] is 4 (Θ(n^2), or Θ(n log n) with binary search). Others: matrix-chain multiplication, shortest paths, sequence alignment.\n\n### 7.4 Backtracking and search\nBuild solutions incrementally and abandon (prune) a branch as soon as it cannot lead to a solution. **N-Queens:** the numbers of solutions for n = 1 to 8 are 1, 0, 0, 2, 10, 4, 40, 92. Also subset sum, permutations, Sudoku, graph colouring. Branch and bound adds a bound to prune. Exhaustive search is exponential, so pruning and good ordering are essential.\n\n### 7.5 String algorithms\n**KMP** pattern matching uses the failure (prefix) function to avoid re-scanning: for \"ABABCABAB\" the failure array is [0, 0, 1, 2, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4]; matching takes Θ(n + m). Other methods: Rabin-Karp (rolling hash), Z-algorithm, suffix arrays and tries.\n\n## Chapter 8. Intractability\n\n- **P:** problems solvable in polynomial time. **NP:** problems whose solutions can be **verified** in polynomial time. **NP-complete:** the hardest problems in NP (every NP problem reduces to them): SAT, 3-SAT, subset sum, 0/1 knapsack (decision version), travelling salesman (decision), Hamiltonian cycle, graph colouring, vertex cover, clique. **P versus NP** is open.\n- To prove a new problem NP-hard, **reduce** a known NP-hard problem to it in polynomial time.\n- Coping: approximation algorithms (for example the 2-approximation for vertex cover), heuristics (greedy, local search, simulated annealing), exact exponential algorithms for small instances (TSP with n = 10 has 10! ≈ 3.6 × 10^6 tours; DP gives O(n^2 2^n)), and special structure.\n- **Decidability:** the halting problem is undecidable.\n\n## Chapter 9. Choosing and Implementing\n- Need fast lookup by key: hash table; sorted order or range queries: balanced BST/ordered map; repeatedly the minimum: heap; FIFO/LIFO: queue/stack; connectivity: union-find; shortest paths: BFS/Dijkstra; optimisation with overlapping subproblems: DP.\n- In practice use the standard library (`vector`, `unordered_map`, `map`, `priority_queue`, `sort`, `lower_bound`), measure with a profiler, and remember constants and cache effects: a vector often beats a linked list even when the Big-O is worse.\n- **Testing algorithms:** compare against a brute-force implementation on random small inputs, test edge cases (empty, one element, duplicates, sorted, reverse sorted, maximum size).\n\n## Chapter 10. Common Mistakes Checklist\n1. Off-by-one errors in loop bounds and binary search (an infinite loop when lo and hi never cross).\n2. Forgetting that quicksort is Θ(n^2) on bad pivots, and assuming hash tables are always O(1).\n3. Modifying a container while iterating over it; dangling pointers after deletion in a linked list.\n4. Using Dijkstra with negative edges, or BFS for weighted shortest paths.\n5. Not handling the empty list, a single node or duplicate keys.\n6. Stack overflow from deep recursion on degenerate trees or long lists.\n7. Mistaking a greedy choice for a proven-correct one (always test against DP or brute force).\n8. Confusing O(·) with Θ(·), or counting only comparisons when memory access dominates.\n","plan":"# Data Structures and Algorithms: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 10 to 12 hours per week (3 reading, 6 to 7 implementing and solving, 1 review with the app's quizzes). This is the most important programming course for interviews and for engineering practice. **Implement each structure from scratch** in C++ (or Python), test it against the library version, and measure its running time on inputs of increasing size.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Thomas Cormen, Charles Leiserson, Ronald Rivest and Clifford Stein, *Introduction to Algorithms* (CLRS). The standard reference; read the chapters as you progress.\n- Robert Sedgewick and Kevin Wayne, *Algorithms* (4th edition) with the free book site and code.\n- Mark Allen Weiss, *Data Structures and Algorithm Analysis in C++*.\n- Jon Kleinberg and Éva Tardos, *Algorithm Design* (greedy and dynamic programming).\n- Steven Skiena, *The Algorithm Design Manual* (problem-solving catalogue) and Jeff Erickson, *Algorithms* (free PDF).\n\n## Free courses and practice\n- MIT OpenCourseWare 6.006 Introduction to Algorithms; Princeton Algorithms Parts I and II (Sedgewick, Coursera, free to audit).\n- Visualgo.net and the University of San Francisco visualisations for stepping through algorithms.\n- LeetCode, Codeforces, AtCoder, Advent of Code and the CSES Problem Set for graded practice. Aim for two problems per day.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Analysis of algorithms (Ch 1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1, Q2. Checkpoint: count operations and solve recurrences without notes.\n\n**Week 2: Arrays, linked lists (Ch 2.1, 2.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3. Implement a vector, a singly linked list and a doubly linked list with tests.\n\n**Week 3: Stacks, queues, deques (Ch 2.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4. Implement a circular queue and an expression evaluator.\n\n**Week 4: Elementary and efficient sorts (Ch 3.1, 3.2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q5, Q7. Time each sort on random, sorted and reverse-sorted arrays of 10^3 to 10^6 items. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week.\n\n**Week 5: Searching, lower bounds, non-comparison sorts (Ch 3.2, 3.3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q6. Implement counting and radix sort.\n\n**Week 6: Hash tables (Ch 4).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1. Implement chaining and linear probing and compare their performance at different load factors.\n\n**Week 7: Binary search trees (Ch 5.1, 5.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2, Q7. Implement insert, search, delete and traversals (recursive and iterative).\n\n**Week 8: Balanced trees, heaps, tries, union-find (Ch 5.3 to 5.5).** Do: Assignment 2 Q3 to Q6. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 9: Graph representation, BFS, DFS, topological sort (Ch 6.1).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1. Solve five grid and maze problems.\n\n**Week 10: Shortest paths and spanning trees (Ch 6.2, 6.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q2, Q3. Implement Dijkstra and Kruskal.\n\n**Week 11: Dynamic programming (Ch 7.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4. Solve 10 DP problems; for each write the state, recurrence, base cases and order.\n\n**Week 12: Greedy algorithms, divide and conquer (Ch 7.1, 7.2).** Do: Assignment 3 Q5. Prove one greedy algorithm correct using an exchange argument.\n\n**Week 13: Backtracking, string algorithms, intractability (Ch 7.4, 7.5, 8).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6, Q7.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, no computer).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can analyse loops and recurrences and state the Θ-class of an algorithm.\n- [ ] I can implement arrays, linked lists, stacks, queues, heaps, hash tables and BSTs.\n- [ ] I can sort with merge sort, quicksort and heapsort and explain their trade-offs.\n- [ ] I can balance a tree with AVL rotations and explain B-trees at a high level.\n- [ ] I can traverse graphs with BFS/DFS and find shortest paths and minimum spanning trees.\n- [ ] I can design dynamic programming solutions with a clear state and recurrence.\n- [ ] I can recognise when a greedy algorithm works and prove it.\n- [ ] I can use backtracking with pruning and know the meaning of NP-completeness.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with no computer.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. For every algorithm write the invariant that makes it correct, not just the code.\n2. Test on tiny cases by hand, then compare against a brute-force version on random inputs.\n3. Draw the data structure after each operation, especially pointers and rotations.\n4. Time your code: the difference between O(n^2) and O(n log n) must become something you have seen.\n5. Maintain a \"pattern notebook\": two pointers, sliding window, binary search on the answer, DFS/BFS, DP states, heaps, union-find.\n","test":"# Data Structures and Algorithms: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. No computer. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Which of these sorts runs in Θ(n log n) in the worst case?\nA) insertion sort  B) bubble sort  C) merge sort  D) selection sort\n\n**2.** Binary search requires the data to be\nA) unique  B) sorted  C) in a linked list  D) integers only\n\n**3.** A stack follows the rule\nA) first in, first out  B) last in, first out  C) random access  D) priority order\n\n**4.** A queue follows the rule\nA) first in, first out  B) last in, first out  C) largest first  D) smallest first\n\n**5.** The inorder traversal of a binary search tree visits the keys\nA) in descending order  B) in sorted ascending order  C) level by level  D) in random order\n\n**6.** Accessing the k-th element of an array takes\nA) O(1)  B) O(log n)  C) O(n)  D) O(k^2)\n\n**7.** Inserting at the head of a singly linked list takes\nA) O(1)  B) O(log n)  C) O(n)  D) O(n^2)\n\n**8.** The worst-case running time of quicksort with a poor pivot choice is\nA) Θ(n)  B) Θ(n log n)  C) Θ(n^2)  D) Θ(2^n)\n\n**9.** Reading the minimum of a min-heap takes\nA) O(1)  B) O(log n)  C) O(n)  D) O(n log n)\n\n**10.** The average-case lookup time in a hash table with a good hash function is\nA) O(1)  B) O(log n)  C) O(n)  D) O(n^2)\n\n**11.** Breadth-first search uses\nA) a stack  B) a queue  C) a heap  D) a hash table only\n\n**12.** Depth-first search is naturally implemented with\nA) a queue  B) a stack or recursion  C) a heap  D) a sorted array\n\n**13.** Dijkstra's algorithm requires edge weights to be\nA) integers  B) non-negative  C) distinct  D) negative\n\n**14.** A tree with n nodes has how many edges?\nA) n  B) n - 1  C) n + 1  D) 2n\n\n**15.** A perfect binary tree with 15 nodes has height\nA) 3  B) 4  C) 7  D) 15\n\n**16.** The best case of insertion sort (already sorted input) is\nA) O(1)  B) O(n)  C) O(n log n)  D) O(n^2)\n\n**17.** Which of these sorting algorithms is stable?\nA) heapsort  B) quicksort (typical)  C) merge sort  D) selection sort (swapping)\n\n**18.** Which algorithm finds a minimum spanning tree?\nA) Dijkstra  B) Kruskal  C) Bellman-Ford  D) Floyd-Warshall\n\n**19.** A topological ordering exists for\nA) any graph  B) any directed acyclic graph  C) only trees  D) only undirected graphs\n\n**20.** Dynamic programming is effective when the problem has\nA) independent subproblems only  B) overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure  C) no recursion  D) a greedy choice always\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (10 marks)** (a) How many times does the inner statement execute for n = 8, and what is the Θ-class? `for (i = 0; i < n; i++) for (j = 0; j < i; j++) work();` [3] (b) Solve T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n^2 with the master theorem [2]. (c) A dynamic array doubles when full. Explain why n pushes cost O(n) in total, and give the amortised cost per push [3]. (d) What is the Θ-class of `for (i = 1; i < n; i *= 2) work();`? [2]\n\n**B2. (10 marks)** Insert the keys 8, 3, 10, 1, 6, 14, 4, 7, 13 into an empty BST. (a) Give the inorder, preorder and postorder traversals [4]. (b) Give the height of the tree [2]. (c) Delete the key 10: say which deletion case applies and describe the result [2]. (d) How many comparisons does a search for 7 need? [2]\n\n**B3. (10 marks)** (a) Sort [7, 3, 5, 1] with insertion sort: give the number of comparisons and shifts [3]. (b) Insert 7, 3, 5, 1 one at a time into a min-heap and show the array after each insertion [4]. (c) Show the array after one extract-min [2]. (d) Why is heapsort not stable? [1]\n\n**B4. (10 marks)** (a) Run Dijkstra's algorithm from A on the graph A→B 2, A→C 5, B→C 1, B→D 7, C→D 3 and give all distances [4]. (b) Give the BFS order from A (neighbours in alphabetical order) [2]. (c) Minimum coins to make 6 with coins {1, 3, 4}: greedy versus optimal [4].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **C** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **A** · 5 **B** · 6 **A** · 7 **A** · 8 **C** · 9 **A** · 10 **A** · 11 **B** · 12 **B** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **A** · 16 **B** · 17 **C** · 18 **B** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 1. Merge sort always splits evenly. 5. Left, node, right gives sorted order. 8. Sorted input with the last element as pivot. 15. 2^4 - 1 = 15 nodes means height 3 (edges). 17. Merge sort with <= is stable. 19. Cycles prevent any ordering.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) Step 1: for each i the inner loop runs i times, so the total is 0 + 1 + 2 + ... + (n - 1) = n(n - 1)/2.\nStep 2: for n = 8: 8 × 7/2 = **28** times; the class is **Θ(n^2)**.\n(b) Step 1: a = 2, b = 2, f(n) = n^2, so d = 2 and log_b a = log_2 2 = 1.\nStep 2: d = 2 > 1: the top-level work dominates, so **Θ(n^2)**.\n(c) Step 1: each doubling copies the current elements: 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + n/2 < n copies in total (a geometric series less than 2 × n/2).\nStep 2: add the n writes for the pushes themselves: fewer than 2n element operations, so n pushes cost O(n) and the **amortised cost per push is O(1)**.\n(d) Step 1: i = 1, 2, 4, ..., below n (it doubles each time).\nStep 2: the number of iterations is ⌈log2 n⌉: **Θ(log n)**.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Inorder: **1 3 4 6 7 8 10 13 14**. Preorder: **8 3 1 6 4 7 10 14 13**. Postorder: **1 4 7 6 3 13 14 10 8**.\n(b) Height **3** (the paths 8-3-6-4, 8-3-6-7 and 8-10-14-13 all have 3 edges).\n(c) The node 10 has one child (14), so the deletion is the **one-child case**: splice 10 out and link 8's right pointer to 14. The new preorder is 8 3 1 6 4 7 14 13.\n(d) Compare with 8, 3, 6, 7: **4 comparisons**.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Insertion sort on [7, 3, 5, 1] (comparisons / shifts per key):\nStep 1: key 3: compare with 7 (shift): 1 / 1 → [3, 7, 5, 1].\nStep 2: key 5: compare with 7 (shift), compare with 3 (stop): 2 / 1 → [3, 5, 7, 1].\nStep 3: key 1: compare with 7, 5, 3 (all shifted): 3 / 3 → [1, 3, 5, 7].\nTotal **6 comparisons and 5 shifts** (the number of inversions is 5: (7,3), (7,5), (7,1), (3,1), (5,1)). Result [1, 3, 5, 7].\n(b) Insert 7: [7]; insert 3: [3, 7]; insert 5: [3, 7, 5]; insert 1: sift up past 3 gives **[1, 3, 5, 7]**.\n(c) Extract-min removes 1, moves 7 to the root and sifts down (7 swaps with 3): **[3, 7, 5]**.\n(d) Heapsort moves the last element to the root and sifts it down, so equal keys can jump over each other (long-range exchanges): it is **not stable**.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Dijkstra from A on A→B 2, A→C 5, B→C 1, B→D 7, C→D 3:\nStep 1: A = 0; relax A: B = 2, C = 5.\nStep 2: settle B (2): C = min(5, 2 + 1) = 3, D = 2 + 7 = 9.\nStep 3: settle C (3): D = min(9, 3 + 3) = 6.\nStep 4: settle D (6).\nDistances: **A 0, B 2, C 3, D 6**.\n(b) BFS from A: **A, B, C, D**.\n(c) Step 1: greedy takes the largest coin that fits: 4, then the remaining 2 needs 1 + 1: 4 + 1 + 1 = **3 coins**.\nStep 2: the optimum is 3 + 3 = **2 coins** (dp: dp[0..6] = 0, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 2).\nGreedy fails for this coin system because the greedy choice property does not hold; dynamic programming computes the optimum.\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Which of these sorts runs in Θ(n log n) in the worst case?","options":["insertion sort","bubble sort","merge sort","selection sort"],"answer":2,"explain":"Merge sort always splits evenly.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Binary search requires the data to be","options":["unique","sorted","in a linked list","integers only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A stack follows the rule","options":["first in, first out","last in, first out","random access","priority order"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A queue follows the rule","options":["first in, first out","last in, first out","largest first","smallest first"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The inorder traversal of a binary search tree visits the keys","options":["in descending order","in sorted ascending order","level by level","in random order"],"answer":1,"explain":"Left, node, right gives sorted order.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Accessing the k-th element of an array takes","options":["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n)","O(k^2)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Inserting at the head of a singly linked list takes","options":["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n)","O(n^2)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The worst-case running time of quicksort with a poor pivot choice is","options":["Θ(n)","Θ(n log n)","Θ(n^2)","Θ(2^n)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sorted input with the last element as pivot.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Reading the minimum of a min-heap takes","options":["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n)","O(n log n)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The average-case lookup time in a hash table with a good hash function is","options":["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n)","O(n^2)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Breadth-first search uses","options":["a stack","a queue","a heap","a hash table only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Depth-first search is naturally implemented with","options":["a queue","a stack or recursion","a heap","a sorted array"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Dijkstra's algorithm requires edge weights to be","options":["integers","non-negative","distinct","negative"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A tree with n nodes has how many edges?","options":["n","n - 1","n + 1","2n"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A perfect binary tree with 15 nodes has height","options":["3","4","7","15"],"answer":0,"explain":"2^4 - 1 = 15 nodes means height 3 (edges).","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The best case of insertion sort (already sorted input) is","options":["O(1)","O(n)","O(n log n)","O(n^2)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which of these sorting algorithms is stable?","options":["heapsort","quicksort (typical)","merge sort","selection sort (swapping)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Merge sort with <= is stable.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which algorithm finds a minimum spanning tree?","options":["Dijkstra","Kruskal","Bellman-Ford","Floyd-Warshall"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A topological ordering exists for","options":["any graph","any directed acyclic graph","only trees","only undirected graphs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cycles prevent any ordering.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Dynamic programming is effective when the problem has","options":["independent subproblems only","overlapping subproblems and optimal substructure","no recursion","a greedy choice always"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Two nested loops, each running n times, give a running time of","options":["Θ(n)","Θ(n log n)","Θ(n^2)","Θ(2^n)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Building a binary heap from n arbitrary elements with Floyd's method takes","options":["Θ(n)","Θ(n log n)","Θ(n^2)","Θ(log n)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The maximum number of probes of binary search on 1000 sorted items is about","options":["3","10","100","1000"],"answer":1,"explain":"Binary search halves the range each probe, so at most ⌊log2 1000⌋ + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10 probes.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The load factor of a hash table with 50 slots holding 100 keys (chaining) is","options":["0.5","1","2","50"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The height of an AVL tree with n nodes is","options":["Θ(1)","Θ(log n)","Θ(n)","Θ(n log n)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In an unweighted graph, the shortest path (fewest edges) from a source is found by","options":["DFS","BFS","Kruskal","topological sort"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Kruskal's algorithm detects cycles with","options":["a queue","union-find","a stack","binary search"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The recurrence T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n solves to","options":["Θ(n)","Θ(n log n)","Θ(n^2)","Θ(log n)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Dynamic programming differs from divide and conquer in that its subproblems","options":["are independent","overlap","are always smaller by half","are unsolvable"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The inorder traversal of a BST produces","options":["sorted order","reverse order","level order","random order"],"answer":0,"explain":"4. 100/50. 8. Master theorem, equal case.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Counting sort of n integers in the range 0..k runs in","options":["Θ(n log n)","Θ(n + k)","Θ(n k)","Θ(k log n)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which of these is a stable sort?","options":["heapsort","merge sort","selection sort (swap-based)","quicksort (typical)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A topological sort is possible for","options":["all graphs","directed acyclic graphs","undirected graphs","complete graphs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A circular array queue uses indices computed","options":["modulo the capacity","by sorting","randomly","by hashing"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Quicksort with the last element as pivot is worst on","options":["random data","already sorted data","data with many ties only","very short arrays"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sorted data with the last element as pivot gives a degenerate partition.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Dijkstra's algorithm can fail when the graph has","options":["cycles","negative edge weights","multiple edges","many vertices"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which of these is NP-complete?","options":["sorting","Boolean satisfiability (SAT)","binary search","finding the minimum of an array"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The memory used by a recursive DFS on a tree of height h (excluding the tree itself) is","options":["O(1)","O(h)","O(n^2)","O(2^h)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The recursion depth is the height.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The amortised cost of vector::push_back is","options":["O(n)","O(log n)","O(1)","O(n^2)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A Huffman code is","options":["fixed-length","prefix-free and optimal for the given frequencies","always 8 bits per symbol","not decodable"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
